            <ArticleTitle>A case of sudden death due to spontaneous coronary artery dissection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>312-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/PAF.0b013e318219c8d3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spontaneous dissection of the coronary arteries is an extremely rare event that occurs usually in middle-aged women and is mostly recognized at postmortem examination in victims of sudden death. It is a rare coronary pathologic finding whose precise incidence, etiology, pathogenesis, treatment, and evolution have not been clearly established. We present a sudden death case of a 53-year-old woman with spontaneous dissection of the left anterior descending coronary artery with eosinophilic coronary arteritis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
